WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.235
Model selection is a resource-matching exercise.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:05.438
Parameter count provides a rough indication of model size,

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:09.709
but the loaded footprint also depends on numerical precision, quantization, context length,

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:10.776
and runtime overhead.

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:15.047
A model that nearly fills VRAM before receiving a prompt may run

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:17.894
out of memory as its context cache grows.

00:00:17.894 --> 00:00:22.520
Leave operating margin instead of treating published file size as the complete requirement.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.723
Quantization reduces the precision used to store model weights,

00:00:25.723 --> 00:00:28.214
lowering memory demand and often increasing speed.

00:00:28.214 --> 00:00:33.196
The tradeoff can include some loss of output quality, especially at very aggressive settings.

00:00:33.196 --> 00:00:37.467
A well-chosen quantized model that remains on the GPU often produces a

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:41.737
better interactive experience than a larger model split heavily across slower system

00:00:41.737 --> 00:00:42.387
memory.

00:00:42.387 --> 00:00:45.234
Test more than one quantization when quality matters,

00:00:45.234 --> 00:00:47.725
using prompts that reflect the real task.

00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:50.216
Choose the model family for the workload.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.775
General chat models are useful for drafting, explanation, and summarization.

00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:55.554
Coding models prioritize software tasks.

00:00:55.554 --> 00:00:59.825
Vision-language models accept images but require additional memory and compatible runtime support.

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:03.383
Embedding models convert text into vectors for search and retrieval;

00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:07.654
they are usually smaller and should be selected for language, retrieval quality,

00:01:07.654 --> 00:01:08.722
and application compatibility.

00:01:08.722 --> 00:01:11.924
Do not assume one model must perform every role.

00:01:11.924 --> 00:01:15.483
Context length controls how much text the model can consider,

00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:19.042
but larger settings consume memory and can slow prompt processing.

00:01:19.042 --> 00:01:21.889
Set context to what the workflow actually needs.

00:01:21.889 --> 00:01:26.871
Begin with a smaller, well-supported model that runs reliably, record speed and memory use,

00:01:26.871 --> 00:01:29.362
and evaluate answer quality against representative tasks.

00:01:29.362 --> 00:01:33.277
Scale upward only when the smaller model fails a meaningful requirement.

00:01:33.277 --> 00:01:38.971
A stable model that fits the workstation and consistently completes the job is the right model,

00:01:38.971 --> 00:01:40.100
regardless of leaderboard fashion.
